  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "sc_pkcs15_decode_pukdf_entry",
    "file": "opensc/src/libopensc/pkcs15-pubkey.c",
    "description": "The PUKDF decoder's error cleanup frees `obj->content.value` (aliased as `der->value`) when `der->len` is nonzero, but does not clear either `obj->content.value` or `obj->content.len`. This leaves the caller-visible object holding a dangling, apparently owned content buffer. The harness subsequently calls `sc_pkcs15_free_object()`, whose `sc_pkcs15_free_object_content()` frees the same pointer again. The root cause is the incomplete error cleanup in `sc_pkcs15_decode_pukdf_entry`, not the generic object destructor where the second free occurs.",
    "conditions": "An attacker-controlled PUKDF ASN.1 entry must reach an allocating `direct` public-key value (`SC_ASN1_ALLOC` stores the allocation in `obj->content.value` and sets a nonzero length), then encounter malformed/inconsistent trailing ASN.1 data or another subsequent validation failure so `sc_pkcs15_decode_pukdf_entry()` returns an error through `err:`. The supplied PoV's decoded PUKDF entry causes exactly this partial-decode state. Any caller that destroys or reuses the failed output object afterward, as the fuzz harness does, triggers the double-free because the failed decoder freed the content without resetting its ownership fields."
  },
